-- allium: 3
-- board-visuals.allium
--
-- Scope: The board's visual language — how a card and a column tell the user
--        which column this is, whether it has the cursor, and what condition
--        the task behind the card is in.
-- Includes: Per-column identity hue and the palette it draws from; the
--           "hue answers which column, intensity answers is it focused"
--           split and its two named exceptions; the column header bar (fill,
--           label colour, uppercasing, the select-all checkbox); the neutral
--           ground ramp and the card surface raised off it; the card frame's
--           shape, inset and colour, and the closed set of state colours it
--           draws from; the card stripe, label badges, message flash and
--           phoenix marker; epic cards as their own identity object.
-- Excludes: The board's structural layout — which navigation column and which
--           section a card lands in, how the cursor moves, the flattened
--           rendering mode, the board's vertical bands and the board-wide
--           filters (see board-layout.allium); the domain model the visuals
--           are keyed on — TaskStatus, SubStatus, Task, Epic (see
--           core.allium); what toggling the select-all checkbox selects (see
--           tasks.allium, "Multi-Selection", rule SelectAllColumn); the
--           per-indicator severity classification the state border reads
--           (see agent-health.allium, ClassifyAgentActivity); the CI-status
--           label vocabulary a badge renders (see feeds.allium, "CI status
--           label").
--
-- Extracted from core.allium (task #4758), which had grown a rendering
-- specification inside a file whose header declared it a domain model, and
-- carried it almost entirely as prose no Allium construct claimed. The
-- extraction typed it: the border precedence rule, the board-wide cursor
-- claim, the scope of the focus exception and the header bar's extent are
-- constructs here rather than sentences. The rationale prose came across
-- unchanged — it is why these rules are re-derivable rather than merely
-- obeyed.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Column Identity and Focus
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How a navigation column tells the user (a) which column this is and
-- (b) whether it currently has the keyboard cursor. These are two
-- separate questions, and the board answers them with two separate
-- visual channels: hue answers "which column", intensity answers
-- "is it focused". Two surfaces are a named exception to that split and
-- use hue to answer the second question — see the exception in "Focus is
-- intensity, not colour-vs-absence".
--
-- == Column identity colour ==
--
-- Every task-status navigation column has an identity colour, drawn from
-- the palette and constant regardless of focus:
--
--   Column   | identity colour
--   ---------+----------------
--   Backlog  | BLUE
--   Running  | YELLOW
--   Review   | PURPLE
--   Done     | GREEN
--   Archive  | ARCHIVE_STRIPE (a muted blue-grey)
--
-- Keyed by task status, not by nav index — see board-layout.allium,
-- "Board Columns", for the (1-indexed) index space this table does not use.
--
-- Archive's identity is its own token, not the palette's generic grey.
-- MUTED is that generic grey — used for de-emphasised text throughout
-- the board — and it is not Archive's identity in any sense: Archive
-- resolves to ARCHIVE_STRIPE through the same per-column identity lookup
-- as every other column, and its stripe, selected frame, top rule, title
-- and scroll arrows all take that value from it. There is no separate
-- archive path and no special case; Archive is an ordinary column as far
-- as identity is concerned.
--
-- Archive's hue does sit in the same family as Backlog's — ARCHIVE_STRIPE
-- is close to a desaturated BLUE — so the two are not reliably separable
-- by hue alone. Nothing depends on their being separable: Archive is an
-- edge column rendered only while focused, so its label is the bright
-- variant at exactly the times Backlog's is the dim one, and the two never
-- appear at the same emphasis. Recorded so that a later change which made
-- hue alone load-bearing (or which brought Archive on screen unfocused)
-- knows this pair is the one that will collide first.
--
-- The identity colour is used for that column's own chrome and for its
-- *task* cards: the header label, the focused column's top rule and
-- scroll indicators, the stripe on its task cards, and the border of its
-- selected task card. A column never borrows another column's colour.
-- Nor does it lose its own — with one named exception, the top rule and
-- scroll indicators, which drop to grey while unfocused; see "Focus is
-- intensity, not colour-vs-absence". (Archive is an edge column and is only
-- rendered while focused — see board-layout.allium, "Board Columns" — but it carries
-- an identity colour on the same terms as the rest while it is on
-- screen.)
--
-- Epic cards are the exception: an epic is its own identity object and
-- takes PURPLE wherever it sits, ignoring its column's identity. See
-- "Epic cards".
--
-- Hue on the board is reserved to these surfaces:
--   - the column header *label*, at both focus states — dimmed toward the
--     fill while unfocused, brightened toward white while focused. The
--     label, not the bar it sits on, is where a column's identity lives;
--     the bar's fill is neutral ("Column header bar").
--   - the top rule above the card list and the scroll indicators (▲/▼),
--     but only while the column is focused: unfocused, both go grey.
--     They sit immediately below the header bar but do not behave like
--     it — see the exception in "Focus is intensity, not
--     colour-vs-absence".
--   - the per-card stripe accent.
--   - the per-card state indicators, which carry their own semantic hues
--     (crashed, needs-input, and so on) rather than the column's.
--   - an epic card's stripe and an epic breadcrumb header row, both in
--     PURPLE regardless of column ("Epic cards").
--   - the panel enclosing the columns in Epic view, faintly purple as a
--     mode signal ("Column ground and card surface").
--
-- Those are the *identity* uses of colour. A card's border is no longer
-- among them: it carries state rather than identity, in a separate
-- vocabulary of its own — RED and YELLOW for unhealthy cards, and a
-- near-white for the cursor. See "Card border: cursor and state". The two
-- vocabularies are deliberately disjoint, which is why the cursor is white
-- rather than a hue.
--
-- Everything else is neutral: the column ground, the header bar's fill,
-- the card surface, a resting card's border, the column separator, and
-- the fill behind the select-all checkbox. The first three are the
-- board's three large flat areas and all three are uniform — the same
-- value in every column at a given focus state, with focus moving them
-- along a neutral ramp rather than tinting them. See "Column ground and
-- card surface".
--
-- == Focus is intensity, not colour-vs-absence ==
--
-- Previously an unfocused column header rendered in a flat neutral grey,
-- discarding its identity colour entirely; only the focused column
-- showed colour at all. That is replaced.
--
-- The rule: on the header label, the column's identity colour is always
-- visible and focus modulates emphasis only.
--   - An unfocused column's header label keeps its identity colour at
--     reduced emphasis — the hue dimmed toward the bar's fill, still
--     unmistakably that column's colour.
--   - The focused column's header label is distinguished by increased
--     emphasis: the same hue brightened toward white, plus bold.
--   - The focused column's ground, and its header fill, are each one step
--     lighter than an unfocused column's, and both steps are *neutral*:
--     focus raises their lightness, it does not tint them.
--
-- The label is where this principle lives, and it is the only surface on
-- which it lives. That is a narrowing: the header *bar* used to carry a
-- wash of the hue at both focus states and was the principle's main
-- exhibit. Its fill is now neutral and uniform across every column, so it
-- has no colour to modulate — the whole hue channel on a column header is
-- the label, and the whole intensity channel is that label's brightness
-- plus two neutral steps behind it.
--
-- The change makes the principle read more cleanly rather than less. Hue
-- is now genuinely constant per column and brightness is the only thing
-- focus moves, where before "a stronger wash of the hue" mixed the two
-- channels together in one surface. Focus used to be signalled by a
-- tinted ground, then by a stronger hued bar; it is now a brighter
-- neutral ground, a brighter neutral fill and a brighter label. What
-- keeps moving is which surface carries the intensity; that intensity,
-- not presence-of-colour, is the signal has not moved.
--
-- Rationale: a board where only one column's label carries colour reads
-- as flat and inert — four of the five columns look switched off. Focus
-- remains unambiguous when it is signalled by intensity, because the
-- focused column is the only one at full strength, so nothing is lost by
-- giving every column its hue back.
--
-- Named exception: the top rule and the scroll indicators.
--
-- Two surfaces do not follow the principle above, and they are named here
-- rather than left to be discovered. The thin rule above a column's card
-- list, and that column's scroll indicators (▲/▼), take the identity hue
-- while the column is focused and drop to a flat neutral grey while it is
-- not. That is colour-vs-absence — precisely the mechanism this
-- subsection replaced on the header label — and it is deliberate: those two
-- surfaces signal focus by *gaining* hue rather than by intensifying it.
--
-- The reason is size, not oversight. A top rule is a one-cell-tall
-- hairline, and a dimmed hue at that size is not distinguishable from
-- grey in practice, so the reduced-emphasis treatment that works on a
-- text label buys nothing here — it would spend a hue to produce
-- something the eye reads as grey anyway. Nothing is lost from that
-- region of the screen either, because the header label sits immediately
-- above the rule and carries the column's identity at rest. The identity
-- is still there to be read; it is only the hairline that stops
-- restating it.
--
-- So the two surfaces are adjacent but not one channel. The header label
-- keeps hue at reduced emphasis when unfocused; the top rule abandons hue
-- entirely. Surfaces that behave oppositely under focus are not the same
-- channel, and an earlier reading of this spec that grouped them as one
-- was wrong.
--
-- Scope: this exception covers the top rule and the scroll indicators and
-- nothing else. It is not a general licence to drop hue when unfocused.
-- Every other identity-bearing surface — the header label and the card
-- stripe — keeps its hue at both focus states, and dropping hue anywhere
-- else would be a regression against the principle rather than an
-- extension of this exception.
--
-- == Column header bar ==
--
-- The header *label* is the column's identity surface. It is what makes
-- the column identifiable, and it is the only surface asked to carry that
-- job. The bar it sits on is neutral.
--
-- Each column header renders as a filled bar showing a focus prefix
-- (`▸` when focused, `◦` when not), the column label uppercased, and the
-- count of selectable items in that column, the count at reduced emphasis
-- relative to the label.
--
-- A bar spans exactly the horizontal extent of the column it labels — no
-- more and no less — and the separators between columns are part of no
-- bar. The header row and the board below it are divided by *one* split,
-- not by two divisions of the same width that agree: the row of bars and
-- the row of columns are the same division of the screen, seen at two
-- heights.
--
-- Stating it as one split rather than as two things that must be kept
-- equal is the normative part. Two independent divisions of one width can
-- disagree in ways that depend on how the division rounds, so they drift
-- rather than break — the discrepancy grows across the row and shows up as
-- slop rather than as a clean offset, which is exactly what a
-- keep-them-in-step rule fails to prevent.
--
-- What is at stake is identity attribution, not neatness. The label is the
-- column's identity surface, so a bar that overhangs into its neighbour
-- puts one column's header over another column's cards, which is the single
-- thing the identity rules exist to prevent.
--
-- The bar's fill is near-neutral and uniform: the same value in every
-- column at a given focus state, with the focused fill one step lighter
-- than the unfocused one. It carries no hue and no per-column variation —
-- it is a third large flat neutral area alongside the column ground and
-- the card surface, and it belongs with them rather than with the hued
-- surfaces.
--
-- All of the column's identity is therefore in the label's text colour,
-- and neither of its two values is the palette token itself:
--   - unfocused: the identity hue mixed part-way toward the bar's fill.
--     Reduced emphasis, still unmistakably that column's colour.
--   - focused: the identity hue mixed part-way toward white. Brighter,
--     plus bold.
--
-- "Mixed from" is a derivation, not a description of two colours that
-- happen to look related. Both label values are computed from the column's
-- identity hue, so changing a hue moves that column's labels with it and
-- the three cannot fall out of step. This matters more than it would for
-- an ordinary rendering detail: the label is the only surface carrying
-- column identity, so a label that had drifted from its hue would leave
-- the identity colour with nowhere legible to appear at all.
--
-- This replaces a header bar whose fill was a darkened wash of the
-- identity hue at both focus states. Two hued treatments were stacked in
-- one surface — a hued fill under hued text — and the fill was the
-- weaker of the two while occupying almost all of the area. Neutralising
-- it concentrates the column's hue in the text, which is where it is
-- legible, and puts the header on the same neutral ramp as the rest of
-- the board's large areas.
--
-- One consequence is worth stating plainly, because it is easy to assume
-- otherwise: on a card, the literal palette token is now rendered on
-- exactly one surface — the stripe. The header label is a derivation of
-- the token rather than the token (dimmed or brightened), the top rule and
-- scroll indicators show it only while focused, and the card border no
-- longer carries identity at all ("Card border: cursor and state"). So the
-- identity colour as written survives on a single one-quarter block a
-- couple of pixels wide, per card. That is a thin thread, and it is worth
-- knowing it is thin: the stripe is the last place a column's exact
-- identity colour appears on a card.
--
-- The focused column's header additionally carries the select-all
-- checkbox indicator, and behind that checkbox sits a single neutral fill
-- marking it as a selectable cursor target within the header. That fill is
-- one value shared by every column — the same neutral as a resting card's
-- border, so the checkbox reads at the same level as a card's edge rather
-- than as a thing of its own kind.
--
-- It was previously a per-column hued ramp — a third one, after the header
-- fill and the superseded card tint — and dropping it removes a whole
-- per-column ramp rather than retuning one. With no hued fill anywhere
-- else on the board, a hued checkbox would have been the only one and
-- would have read as an oversight. Nothing about the checkbox varies by
-- column any more: neither its fill nor its presence depends on which
-- column it is in, only on whether that column is focused and is not the
-- Archive edge column.
--
-- The checkbox is recorded here because the header bar is where it is
-- rendered. Its *behaviour* — what toggling it selects — is specified in
-- tasks.allium ("Multi-Selection", rule SelectAllColumn), together with the
-- distinction between the select-all action and the select-all cursor
-- position (on_select_all), which board-layout.allium specifies.
--
-- The Archive edge column never shows the checkbox, even though it is
-- always focused while on screen: there is no batch operation over
-- archived items for it to select for.
--
-- == Column ground and card surface ==
--
-- The board's ground is uniform and neutral. Every task-status column
-- renders the *same* ground colour at the same focus state: there is no
-- per-column tint and no column ground carries hue. Columns are
-- divided by a neutral separator, not by a change of colour.
--
-- "No hue" is a claim about *column* grounds. Epic view tints the panel
-- that encloses the columns with a faint purple ground, as a mode signal
-- — it says "you are inside an epic", not "this column is purple" — and
-- the column grounds inside that panel stay neutral and uniform. The two
-- do not conflict: one is the board's frame, the other is the board.
--
-- This replaces the earlier per-column tinted ground. Tinting five
-- adjacent full-height panels put hue everywhere and so left hue with
-- nothing to mean; the board now spends its colour budget on the header
-- labels and on the one selected card.
--
-- The header bar's fill joined this treatment later and on the same
-- terms: ground, header fill and card surface are the board's three large
-- flat areas, all three uniform across columns, all three neutral, and
-- all three moved by focus along a neutral ramp rather than tinted. No
-- flat area on the board carries hue. Only text, hairlines and card
-- frames do.
--
-- Cards are raised off that ground. Every card — not only the cursor
-- card — renders on a card surface one step lighter than any column
-- ground, so a column reads as a stack of lit objects on a dark
-- ground. The neutral ramp therefore has three steps in strictly
-- ascending lightness:
--
--   column ground, unfocused  <  column ground, focused  <  card surface
--
-- Note what that ordering is *not*. It is not "everything in an
-- unfocused column is darker than everything in a focused column". The
-- card surface is a single value shared by every column, so a card in an
-- unfocused column is legitimately lighter than the ground of the
-- focused column next to it. The ramp orders surfaces, not columns, and
-- there is no single global chain from "least focused thing" to "most
-- focused thing".
--
-- The *unfocused* ground sits one step below the bare terminal
-- background, so an unfocused column reads as a recessed panel that its
-- cards stand proud of. Only that one step is recessed: the focused
-- ground crosses back over and sits just above the terminal background,
-- which is part of how focus reads — the focused column comes forward
-- while its neighbours stay sunk. Each step must stay distinguishable
-- from the step below it: a difference nobody can see is the same defect
-- as no difference at all.
--
-- The recession is measured against a terminal background the app never
-- paints. The board draws its own grounds but leaves the terminal's own
-- background wherever it draws nothing, so "below the terminal
-- background" is a claim about the dark theme this palette is designed
-- for (a near-black around #1a1b26), not a property the app controls or
-- can check. On a light terminal the relation inverts and the board
-- reads as raised rather than recessed. This is an accepted assumption,
-- not an oversight: it is unenforceable against an arbitrary theme, and
-- a test can only pin the palette's own numbers, never what the user's
-- terminal is actually showing behind them.
--
-- == Selection ==
--
-- The cursor (selected) task card is marked by two things:
--   - its frame is drawn in the cursor white, and
--   - its title is bold.
--
-- Its fill does not change. A selected card's surface is exactly a
-- resting card's surface. Selection is the top of the emphasis ladder
-- but it is *not* a fourth step of the lightness ramp — its emphasis is
-- carried by border colour and title weight, neither of which is a tint.
-- A test that asserts "selected is lighter than resting" is asserting
-- something this design deliberately does not do.
--
-- Of those two markers only the frame is unambiguous. Bold weight is
-- shared with three other states, so bold on a card does not mean "this
-- is the cursor":
--   - a multi-selected card (batch selection) takes a bold title as well
--     as a leading `*` in its fixed prefix, so bold reads as "cursor or
--     multi-selected";
--   - a flashing card applies bold to the whole card, not just its title
--     (see "Message flash");
--   - an epic card's title is bold unconditionally, at rest (see "Epic
--     cards").
--
-- The frame is therefore the cursor signal, and the bold title is a
-- reinforcement of it that carries no meaning on its own. The `*` prefix,
-- not weight, is what distinguishes a multi-selected card.
--
-- == Card border: cursor and state ==
--
-- A card's border answers one question, and which question it answers has
-- changed. It used to mean "this is the selected card" — the cursor took
-- the column's identity hue and every other border was neutral. It now
-- means **state**, and the cursor has been moved off the identity hue
-- onto a colour of its own.
--
-- Every card's border is decided by the first of these that applies:
--
--   1. the cursor card       a near-white (#e8edfb) used for nothing
--                            else anywhere on the board
--   2. an unhealthy card     its state colour, below
--   3. anything else         the resting neutral
--
-- The state colours are a closed set, chosen so that the border never
-- says anything the card is not already saying elsewhere:
--
--   RED     the five hard failures — unprovisioned, auto-dispatch
--           failed, rebase conflict, crashed, and respawn-failed (a
--           phoenix task left sitting in done with its flag intact; see
--           "Phoenix marker"). These are exactly the states whose
--           indicator line already renders a ⚠, so border and glyph agree
--           by construction rather than by two lists being maintained in
--           parallel.
--   YELLOW  the three attention states — needs-input (blocked), stale,
--           and stale-shell (a live backgrounded shell running long
--           enough to look abandoned; see ShellEntry in core.allium and
--           ClassifyAgentActivity in agent-health.allium). Grouped with
--           plain stale rather than given its own colour: both mean
--           "this task looks idle", just for different structural
--           reasons.
--
-- Nothing else takes a border colour. *Dispatching* is deliberately
-- excluded even though its indicator renders amber: it is a transient
-- expected state, not a condition wanting a human, and bordering it would
-- make every ordinary dispatch alarm for as long as it took.
--
-- Why the cursor is white rather than the column hue: with the border
-- carrying state, a hued cursor border would be competing with the state
-- vocabulary in the same channel on the same surface — in the Running
-- column the identity hue *is* amber, which is precisely what needs-input
-- claims. A colour used for nothing else cannot be misread as a state.
--
-- The accepted cost: because the cursor wins outright, a card that is both
-- unhealthy and the cursor shows white, not its state colour. That is a
-- real loss of information and was accepted knowingly. Its mitigation is
-- that the state remains readable on the same card without moving the eye
-- — the indicator line directly beneath says `⚠ crashed` in red. The
-- alternative, letting state win, hides the cursor instead; losing the
-- cursor cue on the card you have just navigated to was judged worse than
-- losing a border cue on a card you are already looking straight at.
--
-- What is now exclusive is the white, not hue in general: at most one card
-- on the board carries the cursor white, while any number may carry state
-- colours at once. The old claim — that the selected card's was the only
-- hued card border — is false in both directions now and should not be
-- reintroduced in any form.
--
-- The rule is uniform across card kinds. An epic card takes the cursor
-- white on the same terms as a task card, with no exemption for its own
-- identity colour — see "Epic cards" for why the purple frame it used to
-- take had to go.
--
-- Hued column chrome (the focused column's top rule, its scroll
-- indicators) is not a card border and is unaffected by any of this.
--
-- == Card stripe ==
--
-- Every card carries a one-quarter left block (▎ U+258E) immediately
-- left of the id, in the column's identity colour on a task card and in
-- PURPLE on an epic card ("Epic cards"). The cursor card
-- uses the same glyph as every other card. The earlier behaviour — the
-- cursor card swapping in a half block (▌ U+258C) while resting cards
-- used the quarter block — is replaced; selection is carried by the
-- cursor-white border and the bold title alone, and stripe weight no
-- longer moves with the cursor.
--
-- The stripe is an accent, not the identity carrier. At normal terminal
-- font sizes a quarter block is roughly two pixels wide, which is too
-- little area for a hue to be legible. It echoes the header label for a
-- reader who is already oriented; it does not make a card identifiable
-- in isolation, and this spec makes no such claim. The header label is
-- the identity surface.
--
-- Note that the stripe nevertheless renders the *literal* palette token,
-- which the header label does not — see "Column header bar". The smallest
-- hued surface on the board is the one showing the identity colour
-- exactly. That is not a contradiction of the paragraph above: showing a
-- colour exactly and showing enough of it to be read are different
-- things, and it is the second that makes a surface an identity carrier.
--
-- == Epic cards ==
--
-- An epic card is not a member of its column's identity. It is its own
-- identity object, and it carries PURPLE in every column: its stripe is
-- purple wherever the card sits — in Done it is purple, not green; dragged
-- to Backlog it is purple, not blue. The epic breadcrumb header row that
-- precedes a group of epic-owned tasks in flattened view is purple on the
-- same terms.
--
-- An epic's purple stops at its frame. A *selected* epic frames in the
-- cursor white like any other card ("Card border: cursor and state") —
-- the cursor rule is uniform across card kinds and has no exemption for
-- epics. Purple identity lives on the stripe and on the title, which an
-- epic keeps at rest.
--
-- A selected epic used to frame purple, and the reason that had to go is
-- worth keeping: PURPLE is also the Review column's identity hue. A purple
-- epic frame would put a column's own hue on a card frame — exactly the
-- identity-vs-state collision the cursor white was chosen to remove,
-- reintroduced on the one card type that had escaped it. A selected epic
-- sitting in Review would have framed in that column's colour, and a
-- reader would have had no way to tell that frame from a column-identity
-- one.
--
-- Intent: an epic is a container, not a task, and it reads as a different
-- *kind* of thing rather than as a task that happens to be in a
-- particular column. Column identity answers "what status is this"; for
-- an epic, "this is an epic" is the more useful thing for colour to say,
-- and the column it sits in is already legible from its position.
--
-- One consequence must be stated because it breaks a rule that holds
-- everywhere else: an epic card's title is bold **at rest**,
-- unconditionally. Bold is therefore not a cursor signal on an epic card
-- at all, and the frame is the *only* thing that distinguishes a selected
-- epic from a resting one. Where a task card has two markers of which one
-- is ambiguous, an epic card has exactly one, and it is not ambiguous.
-- Anything that later weakens the frame signal on epics — for instance
-- giving resting epic frames a colour too — removes the only cursor cue
-- they have. That is the reason the white has to stay legible here
-- specifically: on a task card a weakened frame still leaves the bold
-- title as a partial cue, and on an epic card it leaves nothing.
--
-- Everything else about an epic card is unchanged from a task card: the
-- neutral card surface, the neutral resting frame, the lit frame, the
-- one-cell inset and the four-cell horizontal chrome all apply
-- identically.
--
-- == Task card frame ==
--
-- A task card is a discrete framed box, not a run of text separated
-- from its neighbours by a rule. Every card draws its own complete
-- frame — rounded top and bottom borders plus left and right rails —
-- so that a card is bounded on all four sides and no two cards share a
-- border.
--
-- The whole card is lit, frame included. The border rows and the side
-- rails carry the *card surface* background, not the column ground, so
-- a card's boundary is the change of colour at its outer edge and the
-- frame reads as belonging to the card. The rejected alternative was to
-- paint the border on the column ground and confine the lift to the
-- frame's interior; that makes the frame read as a hairline drawn on the
-- board rather than as the edge of an object.
--
-- The card is inset from the column by one cell on each side. A card
-- does not span the full column width: a one-cell margin of the
-- column's neutral ground shows to its left and to its right, so cards
-- float inside the column rather than tiling flush against its edges and
-- against the column separator. Those margin cells are ground, not card
-- surface — the card surface fills the card and stops at the margin.
--
-- The inset and the lit frame are one mechanism seen from two sides. The
-- lift is what makes the card read as raised; the margin is what makes
-- its outer edge a visible boundary rather than a butt-joint with the
-- column edge, the separator, or a neighbouring column's card.
--
-- The inset is horizontal only. There is no blank row above or below a
-- card: vertically adjacent cards put their bottom and top borders on
-- consecutive rows, with no ground showing between them. So "cards float
-- inside the column" is true left-to-right and not top-to-bottom, and a
-- run of cards reads as a stack in contact rather than as four
-- independently floating boxes. What still holds is that each card owns
-- its own complete border — two touching borders are two borders, not one
-- shared rule — which is what "no two cards share a border" below means.
-- Vertical breathing room was not traded away for width; it was never
-- there, and adding it would cost a further row per card on top of the
-- frame's existing one.
--
-- The frame's colour is not a property of the frame but of the card's
-- condition: cursor white, else a state colour, else the resting neutral.
-- The rule and its precedence live in "Card border: cursor and state";
-- this subsection is about the frame's shape and extent, and deliberately
-- does not restate them.
--
-- The frame is what makes the column read as a stack of objects rather
-- than as a paragraph. It costs one line per card over the previous
-- shared-rule presentation (four lines: top border, title, metadata,
-- bottom border), which is the intended trade: fewer cards visible, but
-- each unambiguously a card.
--
-- Horizontally it costs four cells per row: two ground margins plus two
-- frame rails. The pre-frame presentation spent none of the column width
-- on chrome; a framed-but-flush card would spend two; the inset framed
-- card spends four. Every one of those cells comes out of the title
-- budget, on top of the fixed prefix each card already carries
-- (selection marker, stripe, id).
--
-- That trade is accepted, but it is tight rather than comfortable, and it
-- is tightest in the narrowest column. With the Archive edge column on
-- screen the board divides five ways, so on a 120-cell terminal a card
-- has roughly a dozen cells left for its title. A title that fitted
-- before the inset can be truncated after it, with no slack in between —
-- this is a real effect and not a hypothetical one.
--
-- Nothing adapts. The margin is one cell at every terminal size and
-- every column count; there is deliberately no width-conditional or
-- column-count-conditional branch, and none is wanted. If the title
-- budget later needs to grow, the levers are to narrow or drop the
-- margin, or to show fewer columns at once — the first paid for by
-- losing the card's outer boundary, the second by losing board breadth.
-- The trade is recorded here so that it is read rather than
-- rediscovered.
--
-- == Card label badges ==
--
-- A task's labels render on the card's metadata line, after the status
-- indicator, each as a bracketed badge `[text]` in declaration order (see
-- Task.labels). They are muted grey: a label is context, not state, and must
-- not compete with the indicator to its left for the eye.
--
-- CI status is the ONE exception. Three label texts are a shared vocabulary
-- between the feed and this renderer (see "CI status label" in
-- feed-scripts.allium) and take a state colour instead of the muted grey:
--
--   [ci:pass]     green
--   [ci:fail]     red
--   [ci:pending]  yellow
--
-- These are the same three colours the card indicator uses for the same three
-- meanings, deliberately: a red badge means the same thing on a card
-- regardless of which element carries it.
--
-- Colour is what makes the badge worth having. The information a reviewer
-- acts on is "is this one broken", and scanning a column of identically-grey
-- badges to read their text is not scanning — it is reading every card. A red
-- badge in a grey row is found without reading anything.
--
-- The exception is by exact text match on those three strings and is
-- deliberately NOT a general prefix rule ("colour anything starting `ci:`").
-- Any other label, including an unrecognised `ci:` value from a newer feed
-- script, renders as an ordinary muted badge. This mirrors the lenient decode
-- feeds.allium applies to unknown signals: a vocabulary the renderer does not
-- know degrades to the plain presentation rather than to a wrong colour or a
-- panic.
--
-- Badge colour is text colour only. A badge does not get a fill, a border or
-- a bold weight — the card surface stays uniform ("Column ground and card
-- surface") and the frame keeps its own meaning ("Card border: cursor and
-- state"). Nothing about the badge changes with the cursor.
--
-- == Message flash ==
--
-- When a task sends or receives a native cross-session message (task #4098
-- — Claude Code's own SendMessage, observed via the hook pipeline; see
-- HookPeerMessageSent in agent-health.allium), that task's card flashes for
-- config.message_flash_ttl: a direction-specific glyph and a distinct warm
-- fill. A card that received a message shows an envelope (✉); one that sent
-- one shows an outgoing arrow (➤); a card that did both within the same
-- window shows both glyphs, since sender and receiver are just two rows this
-- rule can independently mark true. Its border is the resting neutral,
-- exactly like any other card's — the flash does not colour the border at
-- all, and takes no exception to the border rules in "Card border: cursor
-- and state".
--
-- The duration is a notice, not a blink. It is set long enough that a
-- human whose attention is elsewhere — reading a diff, watching another
-- agent — still catches the card on their next glance at the board. A
-- few seconds does not clear that bar; the flash would routinely begin
-- and end while nobody was looking at it, which is the same as not
-- flashing.
--
-- Fill and glyph are enough on their own: a flashing card is the only card
-- on the board whose fill differs from the shared card surface, and it
-- carries a glyph no other card has — the sent/received split is expressed
-- entirely as which glyph(s) appear, not as a second fill; both directions
-- share the one warm fill, per the same "no third vocabulary on this
-- surface" reasoning below. (It also applies bold to the whole card rather
-- than to the title alone — see "Selection", where that is one of the
-- reasons bold carries no cursor meaning by itself.)
--
-- The flash used to take the column's identity hue on its border, as a
-- named exception to a rule that no longer exists. What forced the change
-- was a collision rather than a preference: once the border began carrying
-- state, a flash in the Running column would have drawn its border in that
-- column's identity hue — amber — which is exactly what needs-input
-- claims. The flash would have been indistinguishable from a blocked
-- agent. Recorded because the obvious repair is to give the flash a hue of
-- its own, and that is the thing not to do: it would put a third
-- vocabulary on a surface that now has two, to solve a problem the
-- envelope already solves.
--
-- Retiring the hue also retired a standing obligation. While the flash's
-- border was hued, the design depended on the envelope and the hue being
-- co-terminous — the glyph had to last exactly as long as the border
-- colour, or a card would show a hue with nothing to say what it was.
-- That constraint is gone rather than relaxed: with a neutral border there
-- is nothing to disambiguate, and the glyph's lifetime is now free to be
-- reasoned about on its own. This change removes a rule from the spec
-- instead of adding one, which is the main reason it is worth making.
--
-- == Phoenix marker ==
--
-- A task with phoenix = true carries a recycling glyph (♻ U+267B) on its
-- title line, in the same position and by the same mechanism as the message
-- flash glyphs. It says one thing in every column: **this task still owes a
-- respawn**. In backlog, running and review that is simply what a phoenix
-- task is. In done it is a failure — PhoenixRespawn clears the flag exactly
-- when the successor was created (tasks.allium), so a done card still
-- showing ♻ is a task whose copy did not land. One glyph, one meaning; which
-- column it sits in is what makes that meaning good or bad news.
--
-- The marker is not a state colour. Outside done it takes the resting
-- neutral border like any healthy card, on the same terms as the message
-- flash: it is an attribute of the task, not a condition wanting a human.
-- Inside done it is Task.respawn_failed, which IS such a condition, and that
-- is what takes the border — see "Card border: cursor and state", where it
-- is one of the RED set, and it renders a ⚠ indicator line like the rest of
-- that set.
--
-- ♻ is the one card glyph that is not one column wide. Every other glyph on
-- a card — ▎ (U+258E), ✉ (U+2709), ➤ (U+27A4), ▸ (U+25B8), ✓, ⚠ — is a
-- single-column BMP character, and the title-width budget is built by
-- counting characters. ♻ has emoji presentation in most terminals and is
-- drawn two columns wide, so the budget must reserve its RENDERED width, not
-- its character count. Get this wrong and the title overflows the card frame
-- by one column on every phoenix card. This is the only place in the board
-- where the two measures differ, which is exactly why it is written down
-- rather than left to be rediscovered.
--
-- == Where these rules are enforced ==
--
-- Coverage lives in two files, and which one is not arbitrary. Most of it
-- is in src/tui/tests/rendering.rs, which renders a board to a buffer and
-- reads colours back off it — that is the right instrument for a claim
-- about what reaches the screen. Two of the border tests instead sit
-- inline in src/tui/ui/kanban/cards.rs, because they exercise
-- state_border_color, a helper too narrow to re-export and so unreachable
-- from the tests directory; the repo's convention is that such a helper is
-- tested in the module that owns it. Each entry below names its home when
-- it is not rendering.rs, so a reader who searches rendering.rs for border
-- coverage and comes up short does not conclude it is missing.
--
-- Any remaining gaps are listed after the tests rather than left to be
-- assumed.
--
-- In src/tui/tests/rendering.rs:
--
--   board_ground_is_uniform_across_columns       uniform neutral ground
--   focused_column_ground_is_distinct_from_unfocused
--   neutral_ramp_is_strictly_ascending           the three-step ramp
--   board_ground_is_recessed_below_terminal_background
--   selection_does_not_lift_the_fill             selected fill = resting
--   resting_card_border_is_neutral               iterates all five columns
--                                                through the identity
--                                                lookup, Archive included
--                                                and not special-cased, so
--                                                Archive's rendered
--                                                identity is covered by the
--                                                same loop as the rest
--   every_card_frame_is_lit_by_the_card_surface  all four corner glyphs
--   cards_are_inset_by_one_cell_of_column_ground margin width, its ground
--                                                colour, and the lit rail
--                                                inside it
--   card_frame_carries_state_and_the_cursor_outranks_it
--                                                the whole border rule on
--                                                one board — see the note
--   only_the_selected_card_has_the_cursor_white_frame
--                                                at most one white, healthy
--                                                frames neutral, and no
--                                                column identity hue on any
--                                                frame
--   no_column_leaks_an_identity_hue_onto_a_card_frame
--                                                the cross-column
--                                                companion: all tasks
--                                                healthy, so the whole
--                                                board is one white and
--                                                neutrals
--   epic_cards_carry_purple_identity_and_a_bold_title_at_rest
--                                                a resting epic's stripe is
--                                                PURPLE and not its
--                                                column's hue, and its
--                                                title is bold
--   selected_epic_frames_in_the_cursor_white_not_purple
--                                                the selected epic frames
--                                                white, no frame anywhere
--                                                is purple, and every frame
--                                                is white or the neutral
--   an_epic_frame_never_takes_a_state_colour     the other half: an epic
--                                                sharing a column with a
--                                                crashed task frames neutral,
--                                                with the cursor parked in a
--                                                third column so the cursor
--                                                rule cannot mask the answer
--                                                (EpicFramesAreOnlyWhiteOrNeutral)
--   flat_view_epic_breadcrumb_is_purple          the breadcrumb row heading
--                                                a group of epic-owned
--                                                tasks renders its title in
--                                                epic purple
--   a_card_spends_four_cells_of_its_column_on_chrome
--                                                rendered content width
--                                                against column width: the
--                                                difference is exactly four
--   ci_status_badge_is_coloured_by_state         the three CI-status badge
--                                                texts read back green / red
--                                                / yellow, an ordinary label
--                                                on the same card reads back
--                                                muted, and an unrecognised
--                                                `ci:` value reads back muted
--                                                too ("Card label badges")
--   render_v2_task_card_shows_stripe             the quarter block ▎ is
--                                                present and the superseded
--                                                cursor half block ▌ is
--                                                absent, so stripe weight
--                                                no longer moves with the
--                                                cursor
--   unfocused_column_header_keeps_its_identity_colour
--                                                the principle above, on
--                                                the header label: guards
--                                                against the superseded
--                                                flatten-to-grey
--   header_bar_stops_at_the_column_separators    no header fill covers a
--                                                separator cell, at five
--                                                terminal widths
--   select_all_checkbox_fill_is_neutral_in_every_column
--                                                the cursor is parked on
--                                                each task-status column's
--                                                header in turn and the
--                                                rendered checkbox's fill
--                                                read back — asserted
--                                                against a resting card's
--                                                border, which is what the
--                                                spec claims the value is
--   header_fill_is_uniform_across_columns        the fill is the same in
--                                                every column at a given
--                                                focus state and carries
--                                                no hue; all five columns,
--                                                both focus states
--   header_label_is_hued_in_every_column_at_both_focus_states
--                                                two guards: no label
--                                                collapses into its own
--                                                fill, and no two columns
--                                                share a label colour —
--                                                the label being the only
--                                                per-column signal left
--   focused_header_label_is_brighter_than_unfocused
--                                                focus moves the label's
--                                                brightness, which is what
--                                                "focus is intensity"
--                                                reduces to on the header
--   each_header_label_keeps_its_column_hue_signature
--                                                the labels really are
--                                                derived from their hue:
--                                                each keeps the channel
--                                                *ranking* of its source
--                                                at both focus states
--   column_top_rule_is_hued_only_while_focused   the named exception:
--                                                focused rule carries the
--                                                identity hue, unfocused
--                                                rules the neutral grey,
--                                                and no third colour
--   scroll_indicators_follow_the_column_top_rule the other half of that
--                                                exception: the focused
--                                                column's overflow arrow
--                                                carries its identity hue,
--                                                an unfocused one's is grey
--   render_card_message_flash_shows_envelope     the flash's glyph
--   message_flash_envelope_outlives_the_old_three_second_window
--                                                the glyph is still drawn
--                                                well past the superseded
--                                                short window
--   message_flash_expires_once_past_its_ttl      the other side of the
--                                                threshold: swept, and no
--                                                longer drawn
--   message_flash_render_and_sweep_share_one_threshold
--                                                the sweep and the renderer
--                                                read one duration, so they
--                                                cannot disagree about
--                                                whether a flash is live
--   message_flash_never_colours_the_card_frame   a live flash leaves the
--                                                frame alone: no column
--                                                hue, and the only
--                                                non-neutral frame is the
--                                                cursor's
--
-- Inline in src/tui/ui/kanban/cards.rs, against state_border_color:
--
--   every_indicator_claims_the_border_its_severity_earns
--                                                every CardIndicator
--                                                variant paired with the
--                                                border it should claim
--   exactly_five_indicators_are_hard_failures    the counts: five red, three
--                                                amber, and every variant
--                                                in {red, amber, none}
--
-- Those two are a pair, and neither is redundant. The first walks a list of
-- variants written out by hand rather than generated — deliberately, for
-- the same reason state_border_color matches exhaustively: adding a variant
-- forces a deliberate entry, and if list and match ever disagree, one of
-- them is a mistake and the test says which. But a per-variant list alone
-- would still pass if someone moved a variant into the red set and updated
-- its expected value in the same edit, which is precisely how a state gets
-- promoted to a hard failure without anyone deciding to. The counts make
-- that edit visible: it has to change a number that says four.
--
-- Dispatching's entry in that list is an explicit "no border", recorded
-- with its reason — it renders an amber *indicator* while claiming no
-- border. It is the rule's one judgement-rather-than-category exclusion,
-- and so the one most likely to be "fixed" by someone who sees the glyph
-- and takes the missing border for an oversight. An asserted absence is
-- the only kind that survives that.
--
-- card_frame_carries_state_and_the_cursor_outranks_it is the one that
-- carries the rule, and its shape is the reason. It puts a crashed card, a
-- blocked card and a healthy card on one board and places the cursor *on
-- the crashed one*, so the precedence collision is the subject rather than
-- a case the fixture avoids. It asserts amber on the blocked card, white on
-- the cursor, neutral on the healthy one, and no red anywhere — then moves
-- the cursor off and asserts the red it was suppressing appears. That last
-- step is what makes it a test of precedence rather than of three colours
-- sitting side by side; without it, a renderer that had simply forgotten
-- red would pass.
--
-- These four replaced three tests written for the superseded rule that a
-- hued card border meant selection. Those were invalidated rather than
-- retuned: two asserted at most one card frame is non-neutral, which the
-- state colours falsify outright, and the third pinned the flash's hue to
-- its glyph, an obligation retired with the flash's hue.
--
-- Three tests in this section have been written wrong first, in the same
-- way each time, so the pattern is worth naming once: **a test whose
-- measurement is not confined to the thing it claims to check.** Each
-- version passed on broken code while reading as coverage, which is the
-- worst failure a test can have — it does not merely miss the defect, it
-- certifies its absence. The three ways the confinement slipped:
--
--   by metric  each_header_label_keeps_its_column_hue_signature cannot use
--              colour *distance*, because a dimmed colour is far from the
--              hue it was dimmed from. The natural measure is the wrong
--              one, and it fails on correct code.
--   by source  the epic tests must take PURPLE as a literal, not from the
--              Review column's identity lookup, even though the two share
--              the token. Sourcing it from the column encodes the very
--              conflation the test disproves, and would keep passing if
--              epics ever began following their column.
--   by region  flat_view_epic_breadcrumb_is_purple counted purple cells
--              across the whole buffer at first — and the epic *card* is
--              purple too, so it measured the card and reported the
--              breadcrumb. It passed with the breadcrumb drawn in grey.
--              The fix was to find the row by its `── ` rule prefix and
--              read only the title cells after it.
--   by source  select_all_checkbox_fill_is_neutral_in_every_column first
--     of truth  asserted the rendered fill equalled the accessor the
--              renderer itself calls. That caught a *hued* mutation but
--              silently passed a wrong-*neutral* one, because changing the
--              function moved both sides of the assertion together. It was
--              restating the implementation, not checking it. The fix was
--              to assert what the spec claims the value is — the same
--              neutral as a resting card's border — which is an
--              independent claim and catches both mutations.
--
-- The fourth is the one a careful person is likeliest to write, which is
-- why it is worth naming separately from the third. Reaching for the
-- accessor feels like avoiding a magic literal, and that instinct is
-- normally right. It is wrong here because the accessor is downstream of
-- the thing under test: an assertion built from it cannot fail for any
-- change that keeps the renderer and its constant in agreement, which is
-- most changes. The distinguishing question is whether the right-hand side
-- is derived from the subject or from the specification — and a spec claim
-- of the form "X is the same value as Y" should be asserted as exactly
-- that, against Y, not against whatever X currently reads.
--
-- That the accessor had to be re-exported to write the first version, and
-- the re-export became unnecessary once it was fixed, is itself the signal:
-- a test that needs new access to production internals is often reaching
-- for the wrong side of the claim.
--
-- The generalisation: before trusting one of these, check that its
-- measurement can distinguish its subject from everything else that looks
-- similar — the right metric, sourced independently of the claim, read
-- from the right region, and compared against the specified value rather
-- than the implemented one. All four survive mutation testing of the
-- *code*, because the fault is in the test; the fourth passed a mutation
-- and was nearly kept.
--
-- A fixture hazard the border rule creates, worth knowing before writing
-- the next one: a card is *healthy* only if the fixture makes it so. Any
-- test that drives a tick will have an inactive Running task reclassified
-- as stale, and stale now claims an amber border — so a card meant to be a
-- neutral control silently becomes an amber one, and the failed assertion
-- points at whatever the test was actually about rather than at its
-- fixture. This is the cost of the border reading states a fixture
-- acquires by default rather than by asking: "healthy" is no longer the
-- absence of setup, it is setup. Seed recent activity on any card that is
-- meant to rest.
--
-- The separator test checks five widths on purpose. Whether a misaligned
-- header row is visible at all depends on how the division rounds at that
-- particular width, so one width can coincidentally line up and hide the
-- defect entirely — at 120 columns it very nearly does, while the code this
-- replaced fails at 100. A single-width assertion here would have been
-- worse than none, because it would have read as coverage.
--
-- The "by metric" case above, in detail, because the numbers are what make
-- it convincing: measured by distance, Backlog's dimmed label lands nearer
-- ARCHIVE_STRIPE than BLUE, and Review's nearer BLUE than PURPLE, while
-- both are correctly derived. Channel ranking is the right fingerprint
-- because it survives linear mixing toward any neutral — BLUE is
-- b > g > r and stays so dimmed or brightened, PURPLE is b > r > g
-- throughout.
--
-- The superseded three-level tint ladder (unfocused < focused < cursor
-- card) was replaced rather than retuned, because a selected card no
-- longer differs from a resting one by fill at all.
--
-- Not enforced by any test — normative claims above that a reader should
-- not assume are covered:
--   - Epic view's enclosing panel tint: that the panel around the columns
--     is faintly purple as a mode signal, while the column grounds inside
--     it stay neutral ("Column ground and card surface"). One production
--     site, no test. It is the last flat area on the board whose colour is
--     unasserted, and it is the only place purple means *mode* rather than
--     *epic*, so a regression would not read as obviously wrong to someone
--     who found it.
--   - That a cursor or multi-selected *task* card's title is bold
--     ("Selection"). The epic case is asserted — deliberately, since bold
--     is unconditional there — but the task case is not. Low consequence
--     while the frame carries the cursor unambiguously, which is why it is
--     listed rather than urgent: the spec claims two markers and only one
--     of them is checked.
--
-- Both are style claims, which is the pattern in every gap this list has
-- held: text is caught by the snapshots, style is caught only by a test
-- that reads it back. When adding a claim about colour or weight, assume
-- the snapshots will not cover it.
--
-- Every other normative claim in this section is asserted somewhere. That
-- is a statement about now, not a finished state: a new claim here arrives
-- with its test, or with a line in this list saying it has none. Neither
-- is optional, and a short list is only worth having if it is maintained
-- as carefully as a long one.
--
-- Naming tests here couples this spec to them: renaming or deleting one
-- must update this list. That coupling is deliberate, and
-- ./scripts/check-doc-symbols.sh enforces it — a citation that stops
-- resolving fails the pre-push hook rather than rotting quietly.
--
-- The lit frame and the one-cell inset are both produced by
-- src/tui/ui/kanban/cards.rs::frame_card, which paints the margin cells
-- in the column ground so they do not inherit the card surface. Its
-- chrome total (`CARD_CHROME_WIDTH`) is what the title budget is reduced
-- by, so a change to the margin moves truncation on every card.

-- archive_stripe is Archive's own identity token. muted is the palette's
-- generic grey for de-emphasised text, and is not any column's identity.
enum PaletteToken { blue | yellow | purple | green | archive_stripe | muted }

value ColumnChrome {
    -- The column's identity colour. Not a function of focus.
    identity_colour: PaletteToken
    focused: Boolean
    is_archive_edge_column: Boolean

    -- The only surface that renders the identity colour itself, and so the
    -- only place the literal palette token reaches a card at all (see
    -- "Column header bar"). Epic cards are not among them: they carry their
    -- own identity — see EpicCardChrome.
    --
    -- A selected card's border used to be the second such surface. It is
    -- not any longer: the border carries state and the cursor takes a white
    -- of its own, so no border is a function of identity_colour. See "Card
    -- border: cursor and state".
    task_card_stripe_colour: identity_colour

    -- The top rule and scroll indicators, but only while focused.
    -- Unfocused, both drop to a flat neutral grey rather than to a dimmed
    -- hue: they are the named exception to "Focus is intensity, not
    -- colour-vs-absence", signalling focus by gaining hue instead of by
    -- intensifying it.
    focused_top_rule_colour: identity_colour
    focused_scroll_indicator_colour: identity_colour

    -- The header label is *derived from* identity_colour at both focus
    -- states and equals it at neither, so it is recorded as prose rather
    -- than as a derivation — writing `= identity_colour` would be false
    -- (see "Column header bar"):
    --   unfocused label   the hue mixed part-way toward the bar's fill.
    --   focused label     the hue mixed part-way toward white, plus bold.
    --
    -- Neither the header fill nor the select-all highlight appears in this
    -- value at all any more. Both are neutral and uniform across columns,
    -- so neither is a function of identity_colour: the fill belongs with
    -- the column ground and the card surface on the neutral ramp
    -- (BoardNeutralRamp), and the select-all highlight is a single shared
    -- value. The select-all highlight used to be a per-column hued ramp;
    -- it was removed rather than retuned.

    -- Focus modulates emphasis only, never presence of colour.
    header_label_bold: focused

    -- Task-status columns only. The Archive edge column never shows the
    -- checkbox even though it is always focused while on screen: there is
    -- no batch operation over archived items for it to select for.
    shows_select_all_checkbox: focused and not is_archive_edge_column
}

value EpicCardChrome {
    -- An epic is its own identity object: its chrome does not depend on
    -- the column it sits in, and identity_colour here is purple in every
    -- column.
    identity_colour: PaletteToken
    selected: Boolean

    stripe_colour: identity_colour

    -- The stripe is the whole of it. There is deliberately no
    -- selected_frame_colour here: a selected epic frames in the shared
    -- cursor white, which is not an epic-specific value and is not a
    -- function of identity_colour, so it belongs to the board-wide border
    -- rule rather than to this value ("Card border: cursor and state").
    -- Adding one back would be reintroducing the purple frame.

    -- Bold at rest, unconditionally — so weight carries no cursor
    -- information on an epic card and the frame is the only cursor signal
    -- it has.
    title_bold: true
    frame_marks_cursor: selected
}

value BoardNeutralRamp {
    -- Relative lightness on a shared scale whose zero point is the bare
    -- terminal background; values below it are negative. Only the
    -- ordering is normative — the absolute values are a rendering
    -- concern. Every quantity here is neutral: this ramp has no hue
    -- axis, and hue never enters it (see "Column identity colour").
    --
    -- These are surfaces, not focus levels. There is no global chain
    -- from least- to most-emphasised: card_surface is shared by focused
    -- and unfocused columns alike, so it outranks both grounds.
    column_ground_unfocused: Integer
    column_ground_focused: Integer
    header_fill_unfocused: Integer
    header_fill_focused: Integer
    card_surface: Integer
    selected_card_surface: Integer

    -- Focus raises the ground; cards sit above every ground.
    invariant NeutralRampIsStrictlyAscending {
        column_ground_unfocused < column_ground_focused
            and column_ground_focused < card_surface
    }

    -- The header bar's fill is on this ramp too, and behaves like the
    -- ground: focus raises it, and it stays below the card surface so that
    -- cards remain the lightest thing on the board.
    --
    -- How the two header values interleave with the two ground values is
    -- deliberately not asserted. Each surface is ordered against itself
    -- under focus and against the card surface; a relation between, say,
    -- header_fill_unfocused and column_ground_focused would be pinning an
    -- arithmetic coincidence rather than a design decision.
    invariant HeaderFillRisesWithFocusAndStaysBelowCards {
        header_fill_unfocused < header_fill_focused
            and header_fill_focused < card_surface
    }

    -- An unfocused column's ground is recessed relative to the terminal
    -- it is drawn in, so its cards read as raised rather than inset. Only
    -- the unfocused ground: column_ground_focused crosses back above zero,
    -- which is part of how focus reads.
    --
    -- The zero point is an assumption, not something the app controls.
    -- Nothing here ever paints a terminal background — the terminal's own
    -- background shows wherever the board draws nothing — so this holds
    -- against the dark theme the palette is designed for and is false at
    -- runtime on a light terminal. It is unenforceable against an
    -- arbitrary theme: a test can pin the palette's numbers and will pass
    -- regardless of what the user's terminal is actually showing behind
    -- them.
    invariant GroundIsRecessedBelowTerminalBackground {
        column_ground_unfocused < 0
    }

    -- Selection is not a fill lift. The selected card's surface is
    -- identical to a resting card's; its emphasis lives in border hue
    -- and title weight, which are off this ramp entirely. Stating it as
    -- an equality rather than omitting it keeps the old `<` from being
    -- reintroduced by anyone reading the ramp as an emphasis ladder.
    invariant SelectionDoesNotLiftTheFill {
        selected_card_surface = card_surface
    }
}


------------------------------------------------------------
-- The Card Frame, Typed
------------------------------------------------------------

-- "Card border: cursor and state" states the border rule as a numbered list
-- and then spends forty lines on why each row is where it is. The list is the
-- normative part, and it is the part a renderer gets wrong. It is restated
-- here as a type and four invariants so that the toolchain can see it: a
-- change to any row now has to change a named claim.
--
-- Nothing here supersedes the prose. The prose says why the cursor is white
-- rather than the column hue, why dispatching is excluded, and what the
-- accepted cost of the cursor winning outright is. None of that is
-- expressible, and none of it is redundant.

enum CardFrameColour {
    cursor_white       -- a near-white (#e8edfb) used for nothing else anywhere
                       -- on the board. Deliberately not a PaletteToken: a
                       -- colour that cannot be named as an identity hue cannot
                       -- be misread as one.
    state_red          -- the five hard failures
    state_yellow       -- the three attention states
    resting_neutral    -- every healthy card that is not the cursor
}

-- One per card currently drawn on the board — task cards and epic cards
-- alike, because the border rule is uniform across card kinds ("Epic cards").
-- Runtime-only and never persisted, on the same terms as BoardSelection and
-- SplitPane in core.allium. It is modelled for two reasons: the board-wide
-- claim below has to quantify over something, and a precedence rule is easier
-- to get wrong in prose than in a type.
--
-- There is deliberately no is_epic field, and its absence is load-bearing. A
-- type with no way to ask which kind of card this is cannot grow a branch on
-- it, so the purple frame a selected epic used to take would have to add the
-- field back before it could be reintroduced. That is the intent: make the
-- superseded design cost a visible change to the type.
entity RenderedCard {
    -- Whether this card holds the keyboard cursor.
    is_cursor: Boolean

    -- The five hard failures — unprovisioned, auto-dispatch failed, rebase
    -- conflict, crashed, and respawn-failed. Exactly the states whose
    -- indicator line already renders a ⚠; see ClassifyAgentActivity in
    -- agent-health.allium for how a task acquires them.
    has_hard_failure: Boolean

    -- The three attention states — needs-input (blocked), stale, and
    -- stale-shell.
    has_attention_state: Boolean

    frame_colour: CardFrameColour

    -- The precedence table, one invariant per row. Written as four separate
    -- claims rather than one nested conditional so that a failing check names
    -- the row that broke.

    invariant CursorOutranksState {
        is_cursor implies frame_colour = cursor_white
    }

    invariant HardFailureOutranksAttention {
        (has_hard_failure and not is_cursor) implies frame_colour = state_red
    }

    invariant AttentionStateFramesYellow {
        (has_attention_state and not has_hard_failure and not is_cursor)
            implies frame_colour = state_yellow
    }

    -- The row that is easiest to lose. A fixture that drives a tick
    -- reclassifies an inactive Running task as stale, so a card meant to be a
    -- neutral control silently becomes an amber one — see the fixture hazard
    -- under "Where these rules are enforced".
    invariant RestingCardFramesNeutral {
        (not is_cursor and not has_hard_failure and not has_attention_state)
            implies frame_colour = resting_neutral
    }

    @guidance
        -- has_hard_failure and has_attention_state are modelled as independent
        -- booleans, but no card can carry both: a card's condition reaches the
        -- renderer as ONE CardIndicator variant, and the variant decides which
        -- set it falls in. HardFailureOutranksAttention is therefore vacuous
        -- against today's implementation rather than a branch it takes, and it
        -- is stated anyway so that a future condition carrying two signals at
        -- once inherits an answer instead of needing one.
        --
        -- An epic card never claims a state colour at all. It carries no
        -- CardIndicator, so both booleans are false for every epic and its
        -- frame is cursor_white or resting_neutral and nothing else. That is
        -- what "the cursor rule is uniform across card kinds" costs and does
        -- not cost: the cursor half is shared, the state half does not reach
        -- epics ("Epic cards").
        --
        -- frame_colour ranges over CardFrameColour and not over PaletteToken,
        -- which is how "the card border no longer carries identity at all"
        -- is enforced rather than merely asserted: there is no identity hue
        -- for a frame to take. The superseded rule — a hued cursor border —
        -- is unrepresentable in this type.
        --
        -- Dispatching is absent from both boolean fields on purpose. It
        -- renders an amber indicator and claims no border, and it is the
        -- rule's one judgement-rather-than-category exclusion. See
        -- exactly_five_indicators_are_hard_failures, which asserts that
        -- absence rather than leaving it to be inferred.
}

-- At most one card on the board carries the cursor white. This is the half of
-- the border rule that no per-card invariant can state, and it is the half
-- that makes the white worth having: a colour used once is a position, a
-- colour used twice is a category.
--
-- Note what it does NOT say. Any number of cards may carry state colours at
-- once — the old claim that the selected card's was the only non-neutral
-- border is false in both directions now, and this invariant is deliberately
-- not a restatement of it.
invariant AtMostOneCursorWhiteFrame {
    for a in RenderedCards:
        for b in RenderedCards:
            a != b implies not (a.frame_colour = cursor_white
                and b.frame_colour = cursor_white)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Where Hue Is Allowed
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The closed set of surfaces that carry a hue at all. Everything not listed
-- here is neutral: the three large flat areas (column ground, header fill,
-- card surface), the card frame, the select-all checkbox fill, the message
-- flash's border, and every ordinary label badge.
--
-- The list is worth having as a type because the board's colour budget is the
-- design. Each entry was argued for individually and three were removed by
-- argument — the tinted column ground, the hued header fill, the per-column
-- select-all ramp — so a fourth arriving without one is the failure mode this
-- enum makes visible.
enum HuedSurface {
    header_label               -- derived from the column's identity hue at
                               -- both focus states, and equal to it at
                               -- neither ("Column header bar")
    task_card_stripe           -- the literal palette token, and the only
                               -- surface on a card that renders it
    epic_card_stripe           -- purple, in every column
    epic_card_title            -- purple, bold at rest
    epic_breadcrumb_row        -- purple, heading a group of epic-owned tasks
                               -- in flattened view
    column_top_rule            -- the identity hue, focused only
    column_scroll_indicator    -- the identity hue, focused only
    ci_status_badge            -- green / red / yellow by exact text match.
                               -- A state colour, not an identity hue: it says
                               -- the same thing on every card in every column
                               -- ("Card label badges").
    epic_view_panel_ground     -- a faint purple on the panel ENCLOSING the
                               -- columns, as a mode signal. The one hued flat
                               -- area on the screen, and it is the board's
                               -- frame rather than a column ground — see
                               -- "Column ground and card surface", where the
                               -- distinction is made and the fact that no
                               -- test covers it is recorded.
}

-- The scope of the named exception under "Focus is intensity, not
-- colour-vs-absence", as a claim rather than as a paragraph. Two surfaces
-- signal focus by GAINING hue and drop to a flat neutral grey when unfocused;
-- every other hued surface keeps its hue at both focus states.
--
-- Typing it is the point. The prose says "this exception covers the top rule
-- and the scroll indicators and nothing else. It is not a general licence to
-- drop hue when unfocused" — a scope statement, which is exactly the kind of
-- sentence a later edit widens without noticing. As an equality it cannot be
-- widened by one surface at a time.
value HuedSurfaceFocusBehaviour {
    surface: HuedSurface
    keeps_hue_when_unfocused: Boolean

    invariant OnlyTheHairlinesDropHue {
        keeps_hue_when_unfocused = (surface != column_top_rule
            and surface != column_scroll_indicator)
    }

    @guidance
        -- The reason the two hairlines are the exception is size, not
        -- oversight: a dimmed hue on a one-cell-tall rule is not
        -- distinguishable from grey, so the reduced-emphasis treatment that
        -- works on a text label would spend a hue to produce something the
        -- eye reads as grey anyway. The full argument is in "Focus is
        -- intensity, not colour-vs-absence".
        --
        -- The epic surfaces answer this question vacuously — an epic's purple
        -- does not depend on its column's focus at all — and they are
        -- therefore keeps_hue_when_unfocused, which is the right answer for
        -- the wrong reason. That is accepted: the invariant exists to stop
        -- the exception spreading to the header label and the card stripe,
        -- and it does that whatever the epic rows say.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Header Bar Extent
------------------------------------------------------------

-- "Column header bar" makes a geometric claim that reads as neatness and is
-- not: a bar spans exactly the horizontal extent of the column it labels, no
-- more and no less, and the separators between columns are part of no bar.
--
-- The normative form is that the header row and the board below it are ONE
-- division of the screen seen at two heights, not two divisions that must be
-- kept equal. Two independent divisions of one width disagree in ways that
-- depend on how the division rounds, so they drift rather than break — which
-- is precisely what a keep-them-in-step rule fails to prevent.
--
-- What is at stake is identity attribution. The label is the column's
-- identity surface, so a bar that overhangs puts one column's header over
-- another column's cards, which is the single thing the identity rules exist
-- to prevent.
value ColumnHeaderGeometry {
    column_left: Integer
    column_width: Integer
    bar_left: Integer
    bar_width: Integer

    invariant BarMatchesItsColumnExactly {
        bar_left = column_left and bar_width = column_width
    }

    @guidance
        -- Coverage is header_bar_stops_at_the_column_separators, which checks
        -- five terminal widths on purpose: whether a misalignment is visible
        -- at all depends on how the division rounds at that particular width,
        -- so a single-width assertion would read as coverage while hiding the
        -- defect. At 120 columns it very nearly does.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface ColumnHeaderBar {
    facing user: User

    -- One instance per navigation column. The bar is the board's identity
    -- row: the whole hue channel on a column header is its label, and the
    -- whole intensity channel is that label's brightness plus two neutral
    -- steps behind it.

    context chrome: ColumnChrome
    context geometry: ColumnHeaderGeometry

    exposes:
        chrome.identity_colour
        chrome.focused
        chrome.shows_select_all_checkbox
        geometry.bar_left
        geometry.bar_width

    @guarantee LabelIsTheOnlyIdentitySurfaceOnTheHeader
        -- All of the column's identity is in the label's text colour, and
        -- neither of its two values is the palette token itself: unfocused is
        -- the identity hue mixed part-way toward the bar's fill, focused is
        -- the identity hue mixed part-way toward white, plus bold. Both are
        -- DERIVED from identity_colour, so changing a hue moves that column's
        -- labels with it and the two cannot fall out of step.

    @guarantee FillIsNeutralAndUniformAcrossColumns
        -- The bar's fill is the same near-neutral value in every column at a
        -- given focus state, with the focused fill one step lighter than the
        -- unfocused one (BoardNeutralRamp). It carries no hue and no
        -- per-column variation: it is a third large flat area alongside the
        -- column ground and the card surface, and it belongs with them.

    @guarantee FocusRaisesBrightnessAndNothingElse
        -- An unfocused column's label keeps its identity colour at reduced
        -- emphasis; the focused column's is the same hue brightened toward
        -- white, plus bold. Focus never removes colour from the label — that
        -- is the superseded flatten-to-grey, and
        -- unfocused_column_header_keeps_its_identity_colour guards against
        -- its return.

    @guarantee HeaderShowsPrefixLabelAndCount
        -- Each header renders a focus prefix (`▸` focused, `◦` not), the
        -- column label uppercased, and the count of selectable items in that
        -- column, the count at reduced emphasis relative to the label.

    @guarantee SelectAllCheckboxIsNeutralAndTaskStatusOnly
        -- The focused column's header carries the select-all checkbox, behind
        -- which sits a single neutral fill — the same neutral as a resting
        -- card's border, so it reads at the same level as a card's edge.
        -- Nothing about it varies by column: not its fill, and not its
        -- presence, which is chrome.shows_select_all_checkbox. The Archive
        -- edge column never shows it even though it is always focused while
        -- on screen, because there is no batch operation over archived items
        -- for it to select for.
        --
        -- What toggling it SELECTS is not this surface's business — see
        -- tasks.allium ("Multi-Selection", rule SelectAllColumn), which also
        -- carries the distinction between the select-all action and the
        -- select-all cursor position.

    @guidance
        -- Full rationale, including why the hued header fill was retired and
        -- what it cost, is in "Column header bar" and "Focus is intensity,
        -- not colour-vs-absence" above. The bar's extent is
        -- ColumnHeaderGeometry.
}

surface ColumnGround {
    facing user: User

    -- The board's flat areas: the ground a column is drawn on, and the card
    -- surface every card is raised onto. Both neutral, both uniform across
    -- columns, both moved by focus along BoardNeutralRamp rather than tinted.

    context chrome: ColumnChrome
    context ramp: BoardNeutralRamp

    exposes:
        chrome.focused
        ramp.column_ground_unfocused
        ramp.column_ground_focused
        ramp.card_surface

    @guarantee GroundIsUniformAndNeutralAcrossColumns
        -- Every task-status column renders the same ground colour at the same
        -- focus state. There is no per-column tint and no column ground
        -- carries hue; columns are divided by a neutral separator, not by a
        -- change of colour.

    @guarantee CardsAreRaisedAboveEveryGround
        -- Every card — not only the cursor card — renders on a card surface
        -- one step lighter than ANY column ground, so a column reads as a
        -- stack of lit objects. card_surface is a single value shared by
        -- every column, so a card in an unfocused column is legitimately
        -- lighter than the ground of the focused column beside it. The ramp
        -- orders surfaces, not columns.

    @guarantee UnfocusedGroundIsRecessedAndFocusedIsNot
        -- The unfocused ground sits one step below the bare terminal
        -- background and the focused ground crosses back above it, which is
        -- part of how focus reads. Only that one step is recessed. The zero
        -- point is an assumption about the dark theme this palette is
        -- designed for, not something the app controls — see
        -- BoardNeutralRamp's GroundIsRecessedBelowTerminalBackground.

    @guarantee EpicPanelTintIsTheFrameNotTheBoard
        -- Epic view tints the panel ENCLOSING the columns a faint purple as a
        -- mode signal. The column grounds inside that panel stay neutral and
        -- uniform. The two do not conflict: one is the board's frame, the
        -- other is the board. This is the one hued flat area on the screen
        -- (HuedSurface.epic_view_panel_ground) and the only place purple
        -- means MODE rather than EPIC — and no test covers it.
}

surface ColumnFocusHairlines {
    facing user: User

    -- The thin rule above a column's card list, and that column's scroll
    -- indicators (▲/▼). They are grouped as one surface because they behave
    -- identically and are the board's two named exceptions to "focus is
    -- intensity, not colour-vs-absence": they signal focus by GAINING hue
    -- rather than by intensifying it. Nothing else on the board does.

    context chrome: ColumnChrome
    context behaviour: HuedSurfaceFocusBehaviour

    exposes:
        chrome.focused
        chrome.focused_top_rule_colour
        chrome.focused_scroll_indicator_colour
        behaviour.keeps_hue_when_unfocused

    @guarantee HuedWhileFocusedAndFlatGreyOtherwise
        -- Focused, both take the column's identity hue. Unfocused, both drop
        -- to a flat neutral grey — not to a dimmed hue. A one-cell-tall
        -- hairline shows a dimmed hue as grey anyway, so the reduced-emphasis
        -- treatment would spend a hue to produce what the eye already reads.
        -- Nothing is lost: the header label sits immediately above the rule
        -- and carries the column's identity at rest.

    @guarantee TheExceptionCoversTheseTwoAndNothingElse
        -- behaviour.keeps_hue_when_unfocused is false for exactly these two
        -- surfaces (OnlyTheHairlinesDropHue). The header label and the card
        -- stripe keep their hue at both focus states, and dropping hue on
        -- either would be a regression against the principle rather than an
        -- extension of this exception.

    @guidance
        -- The header label and the top rule are adjacent but are not one
        -- channel: surfaces that behave oppositely under focus are not the
        -- same channel, and an earlier reading of this spec that grouped them
        -- was wrong. Coverage is column_top_rule_is_hued_only_while_focused
        -- and scroll_indicators_follow_the_column_top_rule.
}

surface TaskCard {
    facing user: User

    -- A discrete framed box, inset from its column, carrying the column's
    -- identity on a two-pixel stripe and its own condition on its frame.

    context task: core/Task
    context card: RenderedCard
    context chrome: ColumnChrome

    exposes:
        card.frame_colour
        card.is_cursor
        card.has_hard_failure
        card.has_attention_state
        chrome.task_card_stripe_colour
        task.labels
        task.phoenix
        task.respawn_failed

    @guarantee FrameIsCompleteAndLit
        -- Every card draws its own complete frame — rounded top and bottom
        -- borders plus left and right rails — so a card is bounded on all
        -- four sides and no two cards share a border. The border rows and the
        -- side rails carry the CARD SURFACE, not the column ground, so the
        -- card's boundary is the change of colour at its outer edge.

    @guarantee CardIsInsetByOneCellOfColumnGround
        -- A one-cell margin of the column's neutral ground shows to the
        -- card's left and right. Those margin cells are ground, not card
        -- surface. The inset is HORIZONTAL ONLY: vertically adjacent cards
        -- put their borders on consecutive rows with no ground between them.
        -- Nothing adapts — the margin is one cell at every terminal size and
        -- every column count, and the four cells per row it costs come out of
        -- the title budget.

    @guarantee StripeCarriesTheLiteralIdentityToken
        -- A one-quarter left block (▎ U+258E) immediately left of the id, in
        -- chrome.task_card_stripe_colour. The cursor card uses the same glyph
        -- as every other card; the superseded half block (▌ U+258C) is gone,
        -- so stripe weight no longer moves with the cursor. The stripe is an
        -- accent, not the identity carrier — at two pixels wide it echoes the
        -- header label for a reader already oriented.

    @guarantee CursorIsTheFrameAndTheBoldTitleIsReinforcement
        -- The cursor card's frame is cursor_white and its title is bold; its
        -- FILL is exactly a resting card's. Of the two markers only the frame
        -- is unambiguous — bold is shared with multi-selection, with a
        -- flashing card, and unconditionally with epic cards — so bold on a
        -- card carries no cursor meaning on its own. A multi-selected card is
        -- distinguished by its leading `*`, not by weight.

    @guarantee LabelBadgesAreMutedExceptThreeExactTexts
        -- Labels render on the metadata line after the status indicator, each
        -- as `[text]` in declaration order, in muted grey. The three CI
        -- status texts — `[ci:pass]` green, `[ci:fail]` red, `[ci:pending]`
        -- yellow — are the one exception, by EXACT text match and
        -- deliberately not by a `ci:` prefix rule. Badge colour is text
        -- colour only: no fill, no border, no bold, and nothing about a badge
        -- changes with the cursor.

    @guarantee MessageFlashIsAWarmFillAndAGlyph
        -- For config.message_flash_ttl after a native cross-session message,
        -- the card takes a distinct warm fill and a direction glyph — ✉
        -- received, ➤ sent, both if both happened in the window — and applies
        -- bold to the whole card. Its border stays resting_neutral: the flash
        -- takes no exception to the frame rule, and giving it a hue of its
        -- own is the specific repair not to make.

    @guarantee PhoenixGlyphReservesTwoColumns
        -- A task with phoenix = true carries ♻ (U+267B) on its title line,
        -- saying one thing in every column: this task still owes a respawn.
        -- ♻ has emoji presentation and is drawn TWO columns wide, so the
        -- title-width budget must reserve its rendered width, not its
        -- character count. It is the only glyph on a card where the two
        -- measures differ. The marker itself is not a state colour; in done
        -- it is task.respawn_failed that claims the red border.
}

surface EpicCard {
    facing user: User

    -- An epic card is not a member of its column's identity. It is its own
    -- identity object, and it carries purple in every column.

    context epic: core/Epic
    context chrome: EpicCardChrome
    context card: RenderedCard

    exposes:
        chrome.stripe_colour
        chrome.title_bold
        card.frame_colour
        card.is_cursor
        epic.title

    @guarantee PurpleInEveryColumn
        -- The stripe is purple wherever the card sits — in Done it is purple,
        -- not green; in Backlog purple, not blue. The epic breadcrumb row
        -- that precedes a group of epic-owned tasks in flattened view is
        -- purple on the same terms.

    @guarantee EpicFramesAreOnlyWhiteOrNeutral
        -- An epic card carries no CardIndicator, so it claims no state colour:
        -- card.has_hard_failure and card.has_attention_state are false for
        -- every epic, and its frame is cursor_white when selected and
        -- resting_neutral otherwise. Red and yellow frames are a task-card
        -- vocabulary. An epic whose subtasks are failing says so through those
        -- subtasks' own cards, not through its container's edge.

    @guarantee PurpleStopsAtTheFrame
        -- A selected epic frames in cursor_white like any other card. The
        -- cursor rule has no exemption for epics, and reintroducing the
        -- purple frame would put the Review column's own identity hue on a
        -- card frame — the identity-vs-state collision cursor_white was
        -- chosen to remove.

    @guarantee TitleIsBoldAtRestSoTheFrameIsTheOnlyCursorSignal
        -- chrome.title_bold is unconditionally true, so weight carries no
        -- cursor information on an epic card at all. Where a task card has
        -- two markers of which one is ambiguous, an epic card has exactly
        -- one. Anything that later weakens the frame signal on epics removes
        -- the only cursor cue they have.

    @guidance
        -- Everything else about an epic card is unchanged from a task card:
        -- the neutral card surface, the neutral resting frame, the lit frame,
        -- the one-cell inset and the four-cell horizontal chrome all apply
        -- identically. The reasoning — an epic is a container, not a task —
        -- is under "Epic cards" above.
        --
        -- The two epic tests must take purple as a LITERAL, not from the
        -- Review column's identity lookup, even though the two share the
        -- token: sourcing it from the column encodes the very conflation the
        -- tests disprove. See "Where these rules are enforced".
}
